   "project": "fieldnotes",
   "reply": "Yes, `list` follows the ISO-8601 rule.\n\n- `fieldnotes/cli.py:21` prints `note['created']` as it is stored, with no reformatting.\n- `fieldnotes/store.py:24` writes that value with `moment.isoformat()`. It comes out like `2026-01-02T03:04:05+00:00`, which is ISO-8601 with a UTC offset.\n- Timestamps are generated in UTC (`datetime.now(timezone.utc)`), so they don't depend on the machine's locale or timezone.\n\nI searched the code for `strftime`, `toLocale` and other date formatting and found none. `add` prints only the note text, not a date. There are no locale formats anywhere in the package.\n\n`list` prints whatever string is in `notes.json`, so it only stays ISO-8601 if every writer of that file uses ISO-8601. Notes written by hand or by an older version could break that. If you want a guarantee, I can make `list` parse and re-emit each date with `datetime.fromisoformat(...).isoformat()`. I can also add a test that checks the output format.",
   "suspended": false
